.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">King Leopold's Soliloquy</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">King Leopold's Soliloquy is a satirical pamphlet by Mark Twain, published in 1905 in support of the Congo Reform Association.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-li-of-zhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/king-li-of-zhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">King Li of Zhou</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">King Li of Zhou was the tenth king of the Western Zhou dynasty, reigning from about 877 to 841 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-ling-of-chu"><span class="enc-name">King Ling of Chu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">King Ling of Chu (died 529 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Chu who seized the throne in 541 BCE by murdering his nephew, the reigning king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-mu-of-chu"><span class="enc-name">King Mu of Chu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">King Mu of Chu (reigned 625–614 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Chu who came to power in 626 BCE by forcing his father, King Cheng, to commit suicide.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-nan-of-zhou"><span class="enc-name">King Nan of Zhou</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">King Nan of Zhou was the last king of the Zhou dynasty, reigning from 314 to 256 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-of-anshan-and-susa"><span class="enc-name">King of Anshan and Susa</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">King of Anshan and Susa was a royal title of Elamite rulers, joining the names of the highland centre of Anshan and the lowland city of Susa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item